@meta
id: 05-18
title: Four edits, no arrow keys
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: word-counts, WORD-motions, word-e, word-b, word-w
par: 83
minutes: 10
vscode: eight clicks and a double-click, in one file
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
The whole chapter, on one real provider, with a ticket attached.

    ORD-503  Refunds are recorded without a reason and without the
             amount, so finance cannot reconcile them. The repository
             call is also using the wrong finder.

Four changes, all in `order.provider.ts`:

  1. `refund` takes a third parameter, `reason: string`.
  2. The lookup uses `findOne`, not `findFirst`. Change the name only.
  3. The payment call passes the reason through:
     `this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount, reason)`.
  4. The update writes the amount too:
     `values: { amount, status: 'REFUNDED' }`.

Nothing else in the file moves. The imports, the constructor and the
decorators are already correct — if your diff touches them, you missed.

Par assumes word motions and counted line jumps, and nothing else. It is
comfortable if you pick well and impossible if you walk there.

@keys
| `11j` | eleven lines down |
| `$` `0` | the ends of a line |
| `ge` | back to the end of the previous word |
| `9e` | nine word-ends forward |
| `3W` | three WORDs forward |
| `Ctrl-W` | insert mode: delete the word behind the cursor |

@hints
Work top to bottom. Every target is on a line below the last one, so you
never move upwards, and each jump is a counted `j` plus one word motion.
--
The signature and the payment call have the same shape: the insertion
point is the last real thing before a closing paren. `$ge` finds it in
both. The finder rename is the `ea` plus `Ctrl-W` move from lesson
fifteen. The update needs a start-of-token landing in a brace-heavy line,
which is `W` territory.
--
In order: `11j$ge` `i, reason: string` `Esc`; `j0` `9e` `a` `Ctrl-W`
`findOne` `Esc`; `2j$ge` `a, reason` `Esc`; `4j0` `3W` `i` `amount, `
`Esc`; `:wq`.

@solution
    11j $ ge   i, reason: string<Esc>
    j 0 9e     a <Ctrl-W> findOne<Esc>
    2j $ ge    a, reason<Esc>
    4j 0 3W    iamount, <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Seventy-two keystrokes, forty of which are the text the ticket asked for.
The navigation cost for four edits scattered over eight lines of a real
file was about thirty keys, and you never left the home row except for
the digits.

Look at the second column of that solution. `$ge`, `0` and a count, `$ge`
again, `0` and a count. Two patterns, used twice each. That is what
fluency looks like from the inside — not a large vocabulary, but a very
small one that you reach for without deliberating.

What is still missing is obvious by now: you cannot delete, you cannot
copy, and `Ctrl-W` is a poor substitute for both. Every motion you have
learned here becomes an argument to an operator in chapter eight, and
`d3w`, `cW` and `ge` as a target are the same keys you just used. Motions
first, operators second — this was the half that has to be automatic
before the other half is worth anything.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async refund(id: string, amount: number) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findFirst({ where: { id } });

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'REFUNDED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async refund(id: string, amount: number, reason: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount, reason);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { amount, status: 'REFUNDED' },
    });
  }
}
